course: everyday
lesson: 481
title: Blocking, muting and walking away
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A girl in an online game, «queen_slay», comments under every one of Lily's builds: «trash», «ugly», «quit». Lily reads them again and again at bedtime. Her dad shows her the block button. She taps it, and the comments vanish. «That's it?» «That's it,» says Dad. Lily feels like she's taken off a heavy bag. Blocking is a tool, not a defeat; you don't have to read what hurts you.
explain: Some people try to stop you blocking. Sam's «friend» online says cruel things, then «only joking», and when Sam goes to block him: «if u block me ur a snake and everyone will know» (a snake is a traitor). Sam blocks anyway. Nobody «knows» anything; life goes on, quieter. A girl warns Maddie about leaving a mean chat: «leave and you're next, or else». That's a threat. Maddie leaves, keeps the message and shows her mum, who shows the school.
explain: Muting is the quiet fix. Mia's cousins' chat is one long row about who hosts Thanksgiving, with aunties writing in capital letters, and her phone buzzes 200 times a day. She mutes it, checks once a day, and misses nothing she needs. Lily's dad sits with her and finds, in every app, the block button, the report button, how to make her account private and how to turn off chat with strangers. A week later a stranger messages her, and she knows exactly what to do.
explain: Walking away also means walking away from your own rage. Jake, losing at Fortnite, throws his controller and cracks the TV; he loses the console for a month and pays towards the screen. Connor feels the same heat at a tablet game, his breath fast, and goes out to kick a ball against the wall fifty times; back calm, he passes the level: «Get in!» And Chloe, seeing a player type a nasty comment about another player's skin colour, presses report; next day the account is banned. Blocking is for people who keep hurting you, not for friends in a temper: Sam blocks Jake over a laugh, feels silly next morning, and unblocks him. «Yeah, sorry, I was salty.» When Tom blocks Kieran after a row, Kieran makes five new accounts to message him, and Tom only gets angrier; in the end they talk face to face at school and sort it out.
fact: Blocking stops a person messaging you or seeing you on that app; most apps don't tell them they were blocked.
fact: Muting hides messages from a chat or person without leaving or blocking; you can check later when you want.
fact: Reporting tells the game or app about someone breaking the rules, so they can warn or ban that account.
fact: You don't have to keep reading nasty comments; blocking them is not giving up.
fact: Someone who threatens you for blocking or leaving is not a friend; block or leave anyway and tell a grown-up.
fact: «Or else» online is a threat: leave, keep the message, and show a grown-up.
fact: Hateful comments about someone's skin colour or religion should be reported; silence lets hate spread.
fact: Learning where the block, mute and report buttons are before trouble comes makes you ready.
fact: When a game makes you furious, stepping away and moving your body helps the anger pass.
fact: Breaking things in anger has real costs, like losing the console or paying for the damage.
fact: If you are blocked, chasing the person with new accounts makes things worse; give space and talk face to face.
fact: Blocking a friend in a temper turns a small thing bigger; save blocking for people who keep hurting you.
fact: «Salty» is slang for grumpy or upset, often after losing.
exercise: What did queen_slay keep writing under Lily's builds? | Nasty comments like «trash» and «ugly» | Kind tips about building better roofs | Questions about Lily's school and teacher | Nothing at all
exercise: What happened when Lily blocked queen_slay? | The comments vanished and she felt free | queen_slay found her home address | Lily's own account was deleted for good | The comments got worse and worse
exercise: Sam's online «friend» said «if u block me ur a snake». What did Sam do? | Blocked him anyway | Stayed friends forever | Changed his name online | Told everyone he was a snake
exercise: What did Kieran do wrong after Tom blocked him? | Kept making new accounts to message him | Waited and talked at school | Said sorry face to face | Left Tom alone
exercise: How did Kieran and Tom make up? | They talked face to face at school | Kieran made ten more accounts to say sorry | Tom's mum told him he had to | They never made up
exercise: Mia's family chat buzzed 200 times a day. What did she do? | Muted it and checked once a day | Left the family forever | Threw her phone away | Replied to every message
exercise: Jake cracked the TV with his controller. What happened? | He lost the console for a month and paid towards the screen | Nothing, because it was only an accident | His mum bought him a bigger TV for his room | He won the game anyway, got a new high score and showed everyone
exercise: Chloe saw a nasty comment about a player's skin colour. What did she do? | Reported it, and the account was banned | Joined in so that she would fit in | Ignored it like everyone else in the game | Left the game forever
exercise: Maddie got «leave and you're next, or else». What did she do? | Left anyway and showed her mum | Stayed in the chat to be safe | Sent a threat back | Deleted the message
exercise: What did Connor do when the tablet game made him furious? | Went outside and kicked a ball against a wall | Smashed the tablet on the kitchen floor | Shouted at his mum until she took the tablet away | Deleted the game
exercise: What did Lily and her dad go through together? | The block, report, privacy and stranger-chat settings | How to make new accounts with secret names | How to find people's addresses | Which games give out free Robux to kids who share their passwords
exercise: Why did Sam unblock Jake the next morning? | He had blocked him in a temper over something small | Jake paid him some money | Jake threatened to tell everyone at school about him | His teacher made him
exercise: What does «salty» mean here? | Grumpy or upset | Hungry for chips | Very happy and excited | Sleepy and tired
exercise: What is the difference between muting and blocking? | Muting hides messages, blocking stops contact | Muting deletes the person for good | Blocking only works on phones, never in games or apps | They are the same thing
exercise: Why is an «or else» message a threat? | It says something bad will happen if you don't obey | It's a friendly reminder to do your homework on time | It's just a joke word | It means please, only said a bit more strongly
test: «if u block me everyone'll know ur a snake». What's the truth? | It's just pressure, block anyway | You can never block anyone after that | Everyone really will find out | Snakes can't use phones
test: «I keep reading her comments, I can't stop». What's the quick fix? | Block her, you don't have to read it | Read them twice more until it stops hurting | Reply to every comment she writes about you | Post her comments everywhere so people see
test: «Tom blocked me so I made 5 new accounts, bro just unblock me». What's better? | Stop, give space and talk in person | Make ten more accounts until he gives in | Get your mates to message him for you | Report Tom to the game for blocking you
test: «the chat's bare buzzin with aunties in caps, I'm gonna leave the family». (bare buzzin means very busy.) Gentler fix? | Mute it and check once a day | Leave the family chat in a huff | Shout back in capital letters | Throw the phone in a drawer forever
test: Jake: «I'm so tilted I'm gonna lob this controller». (tilted means angry after losing.) What should he do? | Put it down and walk away for a bit | Throw it at the TV | Keep playing angrier till he finally wins one | Throw it on the sofa where it can't break
test: Someone types a nasty comment about a player's skin colour. «It's not my business». Is it? | Report it, it takes ten seconds and helps | No, only grown-ups are allowed to report | Yes, ignoring hate always makes it stop by itself | Join in so you aren't next
test: What would probably happen if Lily only learned the block button after a stranger messaged her? | She'd panic and not know what to do | She'd be perfectly ready for it anyway | The stranger would block her first | Nothing bad can ever happen online
test: «leave and you're next, or else 😡». Maddie wants to leave. What's the right order? | Leave, keep the message, show a grown-up | Stay in the chat forever | Delete the message, leave, and say nothing to anyone | Send an «or else» back
test: «I blocked my best mate cos he laughed at my score». Good use of blocking? | No, block people who keep hurting you, not friends in a temper | Yes, block anyone at all who laughs at you | Yes, and never unblock | No, you should report him to the game as well for laughing
test: In a cartoon, the hero smashes the game when losing and everyone laughs. In real life? | Smashing things costs you, so step away instead | It's funny and costs nothing at all | The game gives you a win | Parents always buy a brand-new one the very next day
test: Connor comes back from kicking the ball and passes the level. Why did it help? | Moving burns off the anger so you can think | The ball somehow taught him the level | The tablet cooled down while he was outside | Kicking makes you cleverer
exam: Identify the principal function of the «block» facility within an online application. | To prevent a given person from contacting the user | To inform the police of an offence committed online | To delete the user's own account | To publish the other person's messages to all users
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the message «leave and you're next, or else»? | It constitutes a threat which ought to be preserved and reported to an adult | It constitutes a friendly reminder | It is a jest of no consequence whatsoever to the recipient | It obliges the recipient to remain in the chat indefinitely thereafter
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate response of a child who observes, in an online game, a hateful remark concerning another player's skin colour. | To report the remark by means of the report facility | To disregard it in common with all the other players | To repeat the remark to other players | To retaliate with a similar remark concerning the author
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child who experiences intense anger whilst playing a game. | Withdrawal from the screen and physical activity assist in dissipating it | Continued play invariably reduces the anger | The striking of objects is a harmless and recommended remedy for such anger | Anger of this kind cannot be relieved
